import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Jobs, cron and email",
	description: "Background work on the rocx server platform: jobs queued in Postgres and run by workers until they succeed, schedules, and email over SMTP, or an outbox while developing.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "jobs", label: "A job" },
		{ id: "queue", label: "Queueing one" },
		{ id: "workers", label: "Workers, and trying again" },
		{ id: "options", label: "Options, keys, listing" },
		{ id: "page", label: "The jobs page" },
		{ id: "cron", label: "On a schedule" },
		{ id: "email", label: "Email" },
		{ id: "mailbox", label: "The dev mailbox" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · jobs, cron and email"
				title="Work that runs later, and again until it is done"
			>
				Some work shouldn't hold up a request: sending a welcome mail, a nightly cleanup, a call to a slow
				service. A job is that work, queued as a row in Postgres, run by the server's workers on threads of
				their own, and run again if it fails. A schedule queues one when its time comes. Email goes out over
				SMTP, best from a job, so a mail server that doesn't answer is tried again.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				All of it is the platform's Roc (<code>server/platform/JobWorker.roc</code>, <code>Cron.roc</code>,
				<code>Smtp.roc</code>), on what the host gives every call: Postgres, background loops, sleeping until
				woken, isolated calls and sockets (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#layers"}>what is Rust, what is
				Roc</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="jobs">A job</Heading>
			<p>
				A kind of job has a name and what it does with its input, which is any type Roc can write as JSON:
				<code>Ok({"{}"})</code> when it is done, <code>Err(why)</code> when it isn't.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The app tells the platform which kinds its workers run, with <code>Job.handler</code>, in
				<code>program.jobs</code> (<a href={"${Paths.platform_}#app"}>an app</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="queue">Queueing one</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_enqueue.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_enqueue.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Job.enqueue!(kind, input, { when })"}</code> queues it, to run <code>Now</code>, or
				<code>At(ms)</code>: not before that moment, in milliseconds since the epoch, as
				<code>Time.now_millis!</code> tells it (a moment past is now). The options are a record, so more
				can be added without every call changing; Roc has no optional arguments, so a call says each of
				them. The input is written as JSON, and read back as the kind's type when it runs. Queued inside <code>Db.transaction!</code>, a job is a row in the transaction: it runs only
				if the transaction commits (<a href={"${Paths.platform_postgres}#transactions"}>transactions</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="workers">Workers, and trying again</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Taken once.</strong> Workers (<code>--job-workers</code>, 4; none if the app lists no jobs)
					take the next job due with <code>for update skip locked</code>, so no job is taken twice, and run it
					in an isolated call: a crash in a job is that job's failure, not the worker's. They take only the
					kinds the app lists, so apps can share a database.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Woken, not polling.</strong> Queueing a job sends Postgres a <code>NOTIFY</code>, delivered
					when its transaction commits, and that wakes the workers; otherwise a worker sleeps until the next
					delayed job or retry falls due.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Again, and later.</strong> A job that fails (an <code>Err</code>, a crash, or its time
					limit passing) runs again after <code>--job-retry-ms</code> (1000), then twice that, and so on, as
					many attempts in all as its kind says (five with <code>Job.define</code>; <code>{"Job.define_with(name, { attempts, timeout_ms }, run!)"}</code>
					says otherwise); then it is <code>failed</code>, with its last error, in <code>rocx_jobs</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Within a time limit.</strong> A kind's <code>timeout_ms</code> is a deadline on each run:
					the first effect that waits past it (a Postgres statement, a socket, a request to another server)
					ends the run, as a crash would, whatever the job does with errors. The statement is cancelled and
					its connection closed, what the run allocated is freed, and it fails with
					<code>timed out after …</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Across restarts.</strong> A job running when the server stopped runs again at the next
					start. Done jobs are kept for a week.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="options">Options, keys, listing</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job_options.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job_options.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Options.</strong> <code>{"Job.define_with(name, { attempts, timeout_ms }, run!)"}</code> is a
					kind tried <code>attempts</code> times in all, each run within <code>timeout_ms</code> (0: no limit);
					<code>Job.define</code>'s are tried five times, with none. The app lists it in
					<code>program.jobs</code> as any other (an app with routes gets it there by itself: rocx build finds
					both, at the top of a module's block).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Keys.</strong> <code>{"Job.enqueue_keyed!(kind, input, { when, key })"}</code> answers
					<code>Queued</code>, or <code>Exists</code> while a job with that key is queued or running, and then
					queues nothing; once that one is done, failed or canceled, the key is free again. It names what the
					job is for, so <code>Job.cancel!(key)</code> (the queued ones become <code>canceled</code>; a running
					one isn't stopped) and <code>{"Job.move!(key, when)"}</code> find it. Each says how many jobs it
					changed, and in a transaction stays only if the transaction commits.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job_list.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job_list.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Job.list!({ state, kind, before_id, limit })"}</code> reads jobs, newest first (<code>""</code> and
				<code>0</code> for any), each a <code>Job.Row</code>: its id, kind, input (JSON), state, attempts and
				<code>max_attempts</code>, key, last error, and its times in milliseconds. <code>Job.get!(id)</code>,
				<code>Job.counts!</code> (how many of each kind in each state), <code>Job.retry!</code> (a failed or
				canceled one, queued again), <code>Job.cancel_id!</code>, <code>Job.run_now!</code>,
				<code>Cron.schedules!</code> and <code>Cron.run_now!</code> are the rest: what the jobs page is made of,
				for an app's own.
			</p>

			<Heading id="page">The jobs page</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_jobs_page.file}, in part"} source={"${Sources.platform_admins_only.rocx}\n\n# … in respond!'s routes\n${Sources.platform_jobs_page.rocx}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The platform draws a page of jobs, and the app mounts it where only its admins get in:
				<code>{"JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount })"}</code> answers every request under <code>mount</code>,
				behind whatever middleware the app runs in front of it, since only the app knows who is an admin. In a
				route file it is a <code>respond!</code> (<a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#routes"}>every method</a>):
			</p>
			<Code title="routes/admin/jobs/+server.roc" source={"import pf.JobsPage\nimport ../../../Middlewares\n\nmiddleware = Middlewares.admin\n\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount: \"/admin/jobs\" })"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>What it shows.</strong> A console of the queue: how many jobs are queued, running, failed,
					done and canceled; what finished in each five minutes of the last hour (done, done after a retry,
					failed); the latest failures, with why; each kind, with its attempts, its time limit and its jobs
					in each state; the schedules, in words where it can (<code>0 3 * * *</code>: daily at 03:00), and
					when each comes round next; and the jobs, fifty a page, newest first, each with its state,
					attempts, times, key and last error. The sidebar narrows it to a state or a kind.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One job</strong> (<code>?id=…</code>) opens in a drawer over the list: its input (the JSON,
					laid out), its last error, its attempts, time limit and key, and its timeline, from queued to
					finished.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What it does.</strong> Retry, cancel and run now are plain forms posting to
					<code>mount</code>, answered with a 303 back to where they came from; one that can't be done is a
					page that says why (409), and a post another site's page sent is refused (403).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>As jobs change</strong>, it refreshes itself, over a stream of events
					(<code>mount?events</code>), and its times count on (<code>3 s ago</code>); Escape closes the
					drawer. Without script it is the same page, with a button that refreshes it. It follows the
					reader's light or dark scheme, and fits a phone. It needs no components and no hydration, so it
					works whatever the app's pages are made of; its stylesheet is its own.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="cron">On a schedule</Heading>
			<Code source={"Cron.every(\"0 3 * * *\", nightly, {})       # five fields, UTC: at 03:00 each day\nCron.every(\"* * * * * *\", tick_job, {})    # six begin with seconds: every second"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A schedule, in <code>program.crons</code>, queues its job each time it comes round, with the input it
				was given. It is keyed by the time it was due, so a restart never queues one twice;
				and since what it queues is a job, a run that fails is tried again like any other. Cron's parser is
				Roc too: a schedule it can't read, or one whose job the app doesn't list, stops the server at
				start, saying why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="email">Email</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_mail_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_mail_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Over SMTP</strong>, with <code>--smtp-url</code>
					(<code>smtp://user:password@host:587?tls=required</code>, or <code>smtps://…:465</code>; or
					<code>SMTP_URL</code>), from <code>--mail-from</code> (<code>MAIL_FROM</code>), as text, or text and
					HTML. The SMTP client is the platform's Roc, over <code>Tcp</code>: STARTTLS, or TLS from the start,
					and <code>AUTH PLAIN</code>; a subject that isn't ASCII is encoded, a body that isn't is sent as
					base64. At start the server connects to the SMTP server, TLS and all, and logs whether it answers.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Without one</strong>, a mail is kept and logged, so development sends nothing: the dev
					mailbox shows it (below).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>From a job.</strong> <code>Email.send!</code> waits for the SMTP server's answer; in a job,
					one that fails is sent again later, and the request that queued it doesn't wait.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="mailbox">The dev mailbox</Heading>
			<p>
				A server started with <code>--dev</code> (as <code>just dev</code> starts it) and no SMTP server answers
				<code>/__rocx/mail</code> itself, before the app: the email it kept, newest first (when, from, to,
				subject), with a Clear button, and each one at <code>/__rocx/mail/&lt;id&gt;</code>: its headers, its
				text, its HTML in a sandboxed frame (no script runs in it; a link opens a new tab), and the message
				whole, as SMTP would have carried it. The list updates as mail comes. With a database the mail is kept
				there (the last 500, for a week), so a restart keeps it; without one, in the server's memory (the last
				100). <code>/__rocx/mail.json</code> is the same as JSON, for tests: with <code>--debug</code> alone,
				only the JSON is served. Without <code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code>, those paths are the
				app's.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>A time limit</strong> cuts a job off only at an effect that waits: Roc that loops without one holds its worker.</li>
				<li><strong>Email:</strong> no attachments, Cc or Bcc.</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests queue jobs now, later, in transactions that commit and that roll back; fail
					them a few times, and for good; crash them; cut them off at their time limit, on Postgres, a socket
					and another server, leaving nothing held; queue, cancel and move them by key; list them; use the
					jobs page's forms behind a guard; leave alone a job another app queued; run a schedule every
					second; and send mail to an SMTP server the test runs, and to the mailbox, read after a restart.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
